# Prior work and how the study relates to it

Keys refer to `prior_work.bib`. Only entries checked on 2026-11-25 are listed. The papers' details beyond the abstracts have not all been read; the report role should read a source before attributing a specific theorem to it.

## Closest work (the study must be positioned against these)

- **Chin 2019** (`chin2019regression`): the regression-adjustment GATE estimator itself (fit a model of outcome on functions of the assignment vector, predict at all-treated / all-control, difference), with linear-model properties and resampling inference. The study uses it as the primary object. Open: no systematic account of when its error is large, no comparison across interference forms, network families and designs, no data-computable warning.
- **Han & Ugander 2023** (`han2023model`): model-based regression adjustment with generated graph/treatment covariates, block-bootstrap CIs, semi-synthetic and real checks. Baseline (richer covariate set) and the closest competitor for "which features". Open: the error is not explained by a quantity that can be computed before/after the experiment.
- What the full texts of Chin 2019, Han–Ugander 2023 and Gao–Ding 2023 say (read 2026-11-25 by keyword and section, not in full): Chin (Fig. 2) already shows that the treated-neighbour fraction/count distributions under one observed Bernoulli(0.5) draw differ from those under global treatment/control, draws the off-policy-evaluation analogy, requires an exogeneity (correct-response-model) assumption, and says extrapolation "may not be too unreasonable" if the response is low-dimensional — qualitative, no scaling in degree/p/design. Han–Ugander note that the variance of sample means of the features may not scale as 1/n and give bias/RMSE tables over feature sets. Gao–Ding assume overlap of exposure probabilities and study regression estimators of exposure-mapping estimands (not GATE extrapolation). So the extrapolation viewpoint is not new; the quantified account, its decomposition, the design interaction and the diagnostic are the claimed additions, "to our knowledge within the literature checked".
- **Gao & Ding 2023** (`gao2023causal`): regression-based analysis of network experiments with design-based properties. Closest design-based treatment of regression estimators; to be read in detail before the paper states what is new.
- **Aronow & Samii 2017, Ugander et al. 2013, Eckles et al. 2017** (`aronow2017estimating`, `ugander2013graph`, `eckles2017design`): exposure-mapping HT/Hájek estimators, graph cluster randomization, neighbourhood-fraction exposures. Baselines (HT/Hájek with fraction-threshold exposure; DM under cluster randomization) and the source of the designs we cross with the regression estimator.
- **Cortez et al. 2022** (`cortez2022staggered`): staggered rollout, polynomial (low-order interaction) estimator without network knowledge. Different design; an optional baseline where the network is unknown.

## Related methods and theory

- Unknown / approximate interference: `savje2021average` (DM is consistent for a marginalised estimand — shows what DM estimates), `leung2022causal`, `savje2023causal` (misspecified exposure mappings: separate estimand and assumption — our setting is exactly estimand = GATE with a possibly misspecified model), `yu2022estimating` (unknown network).
- Designs: `leung2022rate`, `ugander2023randomized`, `candogan2023correlated`, `basse2018designs`, `karrer2021network`, `gui2015network`, `chen2023optimized` (HT estimator and covariance design under one-hop model).
- Theory under random graphs / linear interference: `li2022random`, `sussman2017elements`, `hu2022average`.
- Graph learners as optional regressors: `kipf2017semi`, `defferrard2016convolutional`.

- Regression-style competitors found by the idea review: `thiyageswaran2024data` (data-adaptive exposure thresholds for HT; abstract read), `shirani2024causal` (causal message-passing; abstract read). Thiyageswaran enters the comparison as a simplified variant (reported under that name); Shirani–Bayati does not: it is defined for panel outcomes and the study has one outcome per unit, so it is cited as related work with the reason (Brief rev. 6). Neither source was read beyond the abstract by this role; `report` reads them before attributing anything.
- Adjacent ideas the extrapolation framing transfers from: limited overlap/positivity (`crump2009dealing`, `damour2021overlap`) and OLS prediction leverage at a far point. Exposure features here are functions of one shared assignment vector, so degree and design shape the leverage; that structure is what is new.

- Honest inference under an unidentified bias (for the sensitivity interval, Brief P6): `armstrong2018optimal` and `armstrong2020simple` give confidence intervals that add a worst-case bias over a function class with a bounded derivative (fixed-length intervals with critical value cv_α(b/SE)); their results show that for centrosymmetric convex classes the smoothness bound must be chosen a priori and cannot be adapted to from the data (read in this role: the 2018 abstract and introduction, the 2020 introduction and its rule-of-thumb paragraph; theorems not checked). `armstrong2020simple` also proposes the rule of thumb M̂ = sup |second derivative of a global polynomial fit| over the observed support, as a starting point for sensitivity analysis over M; the study's B₂ is that rule with a multiplier κ (the same for the range of specifications, B₁). `rambachan2023more` bounds the post-treatment violation of parallel trends relative to the pre-trend (abstract read only; no open full text was reachable), the same logic as κ × in-support magnitude. The study does not claim the interval form: it applies it to the extrapolation functional of exposure regressions (corner offsets c(p), degree dependence), tests it against an equal-width control and on a confirmatory block, and reports κ*, how much assumption each mechanism needs. The non-adaptivity result is the formal counterpart, for smooth classes, of the study's non-identification argument; the threshold response lies outside any bounded-second-derivative class.

## What the study takes from them and adds

Takes: the GATE estimand, Bernoulli and graph-cluster designs, exposure-mapping HT/Hájek and DM as baselines, Chin's estimator and Han–Ugander's covariates as the regression family.
Adds (the claimed contribution, see the Brief): a diagnosis of regression-GATE error as extrapolation to the global-treatment corner, a variance diagnostic (leverage of the corner contrast) that predicts the variance, the result that the bias is not predictable from the data without an assumption (data-computable bias scores refuted; an oracle predictor that uses the true response holds), an assumption-indexed sensitivity interval for it, a regime map over interference form × network family × design × treatment probability × degree, and a falsifiable test of the scaling predictions, with negative results reported.

